<p>A <a href="https://www.google.com/books/edition/Catalogue_of_the_Greek_Papyri_in_the_Joh/FKoPAQAAMAAJ?hl=en&#038;gbpv=1&#038;pg=PA8&#038;printsec=frontcover">letter</a> from the king&#8217;s minister of finance to Zenon of Kaunos, a public official in Ptolemaic Egypt, 256 B.C.:</p>
<blockquote><p>
Apollonius to Zenon, greeting. You did right to send the chickpeas to Memphis. Farewell.
</p></blockquote>
<p>In <em>The Complete Plain Words</em>, his 1948 style guide for the British civil service, Sir Ernest Gowers calls this a model in avoiding verbiage.</p>
